+# Decision: Delivery approach for committed revisions
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit an event revision. Once a revision is committed, connected browsers need to see it quickly — the goal is 15 seconds under normal connectivity, and subsecond delivery is useful though not required.
+
+Three mechanisms can carry committed revisions from server to browser. The choice is load-bearing: it determines what the change store must retain, how the delivery layer behaves, and what reconnect and catch-up logic browsers need. All three nodes are unwritten, so the choice is made here before any of them are designed.
+
+The project serves up to 800 simultaneous browsers with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second and must recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes of disconnection.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **Meets 15-second freshness target under normal connectivity.** A delivery mechanism that cannot reliably show committed revisions within 15 seconds is eliminated.
+- **Recoverable after 20-minute disconnection.** The mechanism, combined with the change store, must allow a browser to recover the full current board after a gap of up to 20 minutes.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Delivery latency** *(major)* — how quickly a committed revision reaches connected browsers under normal load. Subsecond is useful; seconds of structural lag reduce the operational value of the board.
+- **Operational burden** *(major)* — the ongoing cost to run and maintain the delivery infrastructure: connection capacity, failure modes, reconnect edge cases, monitoring. MarketBoard is operated by market coordinators, not a dedicated infrastructure team. Tenet: *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* — neither is favored, but complexity must be weighed.
+- **Vendor coupling and recurring cost** *(moderate)* — whether the delivery path ties the project to a third-party service, adds a recurring fee, and creates a provider dependency for a core infrastructure concern.
+- **Store and browser complexity added** *(moderate)* — what each option obligates the change store and browser sync to implement beyond what they would need otherwise.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Option A — Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers poll a dedicated endpoint on a fixed interval (≤5 seconds). Each response returns any revisions committed since the browser's last-known cursor. The server is stateless with respect to delivery: no persistent connections, no publication machinery. The change store needs efficient cursor-based range queries; browsers need a polling loop and cursor bookkeeping.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Meets 15-second freshness target: **passes** — a 5-second interval delivers within 5 seconds of commit under normal conditions.
+- Recoverable after 20-minute disconnection: **passes** — catch-up is a cursor query; same mechanism as normal polling.
+- Delivery latency: **weak fit** — structural lag of 0–5 seconds is baked in; faster intervals raise server load proportionally. Not subsecond under any tuning.
+- Operational burden: **strong fit** — no persistent connections to manage; the server is stateless with respect to delivery; reconnect is free (next poll just works). Simplest to operate.
+- Vendor coupling and recurring cost: **strong fit** — entirely self-hosted, no external service.
+- Store and browser complexity added: **partial fit** — the store needs indexed cursor queries (moderate); browsers need a polling loop (low). No session or replay machinery required.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest to operate, but delivers structural lag and scales poll load linearly with connected browsers.
+
+---
+
+### Option B — Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server maintains persistent WebSocket connections to connected browsers. When a revision is committed, the delivery layer broadcasts it to all connected sessions. Browsers open a WebSocket connection on load, receive revision events in near-real time, and catch up after reconnection by requesting revisions since their last-known cursor. The change store needs a replayable revision log and a publication outbox; the delivery layer manages connection lifecycle, reconnect, and broadcast; browsers need reconnect logic and catch-up on resume.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Meets 15-second freshness target: **passes** — committed revisions are pushed within milliseconds of commit.
+- Recoverable after 20-minute disconnection: **passes** — catch-up uses the replayable revision log in the change store.
+- Delivery latency: **strong fit** — subsecond under normal conditions; delivery latency is network round-trip, not a poll interval.
+- Operational burden: **partial fit** — connection capacity must be provisioned; reconnect edge cases (mid-burst disconnect, duplicate delivery, missed revision) must be handled. More moving parts than polling, but all parts are owned and observable by the project.
+- Vendor coupling and recurring cost: **strong fit** — entirely self-hosted, no external service or recurring fee.
+- Store and browser complexity added: **partial fit** — the store needs a replayable revision log and publication outbox (moderate); browsers need reconnect and catch-up logic (moderate).
+
+**Summary.** Near-real-time delivery without vendor dependency, at the cost of owning connection and reconnect complexity.
+
+---
+
+### Option C — Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime service (e.g. Ably, Pusher, or similar) manages WebSocket connections and fan-out. The server publishes committed revisions to the service's API; connected browsers subscribe via the service's client SDK. Connection management, reconnect, and fan-out are delegated to the provider. The change store needs reconciliation logic to handle publication failures; the delivery layer wraps the provider's publish API; browsers use the provider's subscription model and SDK.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Meets 15-second freshness target: **passes** — provider-managed delivery is typically subsecond.
+- Recoverable after 20-minute disconnection: **passes** — the change store still needs its own revision history for catch-up beyond provider message retention windows.
+- Delivery latency: **strong fit** — subsecond, comparable to self-hosted WebSockets under normal conditions.
+- Operational burden: **strong fit** for connection management (delegated to provider); **partial fit** overall — publication failures, provider outages, and SDK version management become operational concerns of a different kind.
+- Vendor coupling and recurring cost: **weak fit** — recurring fee at scale, provider dependency for a core infrastructure path, and migration cost if the provider changes pricing or availability. Tenet: *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* — this option is permitted, but the coupling is a real cost.
+- Store and browser complexity added: **partial fit** — the store needs publication reconciliation (moderate); browsers use the provider SDK (low direct complexity, but SDK dependency adds indirect coupling).
+
+**Summary.** Lowest connection-ops burden, but introduces vendor coupling and a recurring fee for infrastructure the project can own.
+
+---
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets (Option B).**
+
+The two major criteria drive the outcome. On delivery latency, Options B and C both deliver subsecond; Option A cannot. On operational burden, Option C is lightest for connection management but introduces vendor coupling — a moderate-importance criterion that weakens it against the *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* tenet (neither is favored, and coupling to a provider for a core delivery path is a cost this project need not accept). Option A is operationally simplest but fails the latency criterion that makes the board operationally valuable at opening time.
+
+Option B clears the latency bar, avoids vendor coupling and recurring cost, and keeps all infrastructure observable by the project team. The tradeoff accepted is owning connection capacity and reconnect logic — real operational complexity, but bounded and self-contained.
+
+This selection obligates three downstream nodes:
+
+- **Change store** must maintain a replayable revision log and a publication outbox for broadcasting to the delivery layer.
+- **Delivery** must manage WebSocket session lifecycle, handle reconnect events, and broadcast committed revisions reliably to connected sessions.
+- **Browser sync** must open a WebSocket connection, reconnect on drop, and catch up by requesting missed revisions from its last-known cursor.
