 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed revisions are delivered to browsers over WebSocket connections managed by this project. HTTPS polling and hosted realtime services were the alternatives. Polling meets the 15-second freshness goal but adds query load and incurs seconds of structural lag. A hosted realtime service reduces connection ops but adds a recurring vendor fee and provider coupling for an infrastructure concern the project can own. Self-hosting is accepted in exchange for subsecond delivery latency and no vendor dependency; the cost is owning connection capacity and reconnect logic. See [`docs/decisions/delivery-approach.md`](decisions/delivery-approach.md).
